- Pi is exact `0.84.0`, uses an owned profile whose sole package is `packages/pi-music-dock`, and loads it through reloadable profile package discovery.
- Both regular-pane UIs simultaneously contain exactly one coherent extension-specific music representation tying the canonical identity and state marker together; Pi's complete marker/waveform/clipped-title/separator/clipped-artist status occurs exactly once. Every acceptance capture proves zoom-on, text read, ANSI read, and zoom-off all succeeded, so a split-viewport read cannot pass as the required wide capture. Each live host process group has exactly one identified owned peer; unrelated baseline rows are contextual and may depart.
- OpenCode's named `Music / Play / pause` action is visibly selected in the palette and changes canonical playback; Pi `/music` causes the reverse transition.
- PID/generation/socket/peer checkpoints immediately bracket every direct/host control, reload, and exit.
- Pi `/reload` preserves exact foreground identity/full commands, complete process group, one peer/status, media state, and healthy OpenCode.
- Pi `/quit` and OpenCode's selected `System / Exit the app` action return their original shells; recorded PIDs/groups/socket rows are gone before owned-root deletion. A submitted launch that cannot be classified or proven returned leaves its owned pane/root/tab in place for rework.
- Post-Pi OpenCode control works and the second action restores the exact original canonical snapshot.
- No recorded owned peer remains after both normal exits. PID `45621`, process owner, command, socket tuple/ownership/mode, generation, and unrelated panes remain unchanged. Unrelated peer rows are never signaled/cleaned and need not equal baseline. Only owned roots/tab/panes disappear.
- No shortcut-encoded music action, OpenCode `/quit`, `ctrl+c`, signal, protected/unrelated cleanup, or final idle claim occurs.
- On failure, independent best-effort probes retain before/after process, full-command, group, peer, and UI evidence even if protected checks fail. Ownership flags are not cleared unless shell return, recorded PID disappearance, empty group, owned-peer disappearance, and post-exit checkpoint all succeed; regardless, error recovery deletes no root and closes no owned tab/pane. Exact IDs and the complete transcript are copied into the dispatched coder-result rather than left only in a temporary log.
